@prefix triage: <urn:atm:triage:> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:atm:triage:finding/AW-POOL-V4>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AW-POOL-V4" ;
  triage:title "graft-receiver-endpoint and storage/nudge router reads run on the writer-side control_path_pool with no read-modify-write pairing" ;
  triage:description "fenix writer-pool audit V4: crates/atm-storage-rusqlite/src/graft_receiver_endpoint_store.rs:179 and storage_and_nudge_router.rs:774-787. Applying Rand's RMW deciding test (01M1T3QFQ3MXYB5NM5VXEG41EP): these reads (graft-receiver endpoint lookup, router-side nudge/state fan-out reads) are not paired with a subsequent conditional write against the same value in the same transaction at the cited sites; a stale read here produces at worst a slightly-behind endpoint/nudge routing decision, which the don't-care-race principle (01M1T45T3DAW53W7N5BQ83GJG3) covers. Acceptance: move to the single read pool (post AW-POOL-V9); no write-path read remains at these sites. Severity important rather than blocking (fenix's proposal, unchallenged by any Rand ruling specific to V4): lower request-volume path than V1/V3/V7, and not on a foundational hot loop." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "step2_verdict" ;
  triage:category "concurrency-architecture" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "workspace" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-06T01:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedAt "2026-09-06T18:45:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AWWriterPoolAudit ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-05T23:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-POOL-V4/endpoint-store> ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW-POOL-V4/router> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

# quality-mgr, 2026-09-06, phase-aw-gate-closeout-fix-round2 (PR #1254 @ 2bc491f9a):
# graft_receiver_lookup (storage_and_nudge_router.rs) -- the single non-RMW
# read shared by both occurrences -- no longer runs inside
# control_path_sync_bridge.run(...). validate_graft_receiver_member runs
# directly (RAM), then store.lookup_with_deadline runs via a bare
# tokio::task::spawn_blocking, which itself calls SharedDb::read_with_deadline
# -> self.read_pool.submit_blocking(...) (the genuine deadline-bounded reader
# pool, verified by direct read of shared_db.rs:214-238) -- never the writer
# pool. Confirmed the sibling RMW operations on this same store
# (graft_receiver_register/refresh/unregister) correctly remain on
# control_path_sync_bridge, since those do write. A new architecture
# regression guard (aw_pool_graft_receiver_lookup_uses_the_deadline_bounded_
# reader_lane, boundary_enforcement.rs) mechanically asserts
# graft_receiver_lookup contains spawn_blocking+lookup_with_deadline and
# does NOT contain control_path_sync_bridge.
#
# quality-mgr, 2026-09-06, rust-qa-agent verification at ecc2e118f (branch
# later fast-forward-merged with develop -> 95f8594d8, no diff to reviewed
# files): `cargo test -p atm-architecture --test boundary_enforcement`
# 95/95 PASS, including aw_pool_graft_receiver_lookup_uses_the_deadline_
# bounded_reader_lane and av3_control_path_bridge_call_sites_are_the_exact_
# residual_set_after_rename (residual set no longer includes
# graft_receiver_lookup, reconciled by commit ecc2e118f). `cargo clippy -p
# atm-architecture -p atm-http-runtime --tests -- -D warnings` clean. This
# finding's stated acceptance ("no write-path read remains at these sites")
# is satisfied. The same reviewer separately flagged (non-blocking,
# important) that graft_receiver_lookup bridges via spawn_blocking+
# lookup_with_deadline->submit_blocking rather than the reader pool's
# native async submit path used by sibling _async methods elsewhere in
# atm-storage-rusqlite -- filed as a new finding for team-lead's triage,
# not a reopening of this record: this record's own acceptance criterion
# (writer-pool removal) does not require the async-native submit path.
